00:00:41.897 --> 00:00:43.730
And fourth, we're going
to talk a little bit

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.040
about the operations
of functions.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.750
So first, a quick
background on testing.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.990
Testing can be defined as
sort of two separate things.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:58.570
So we're asking two
high-level questions here.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:01.530
First of all, does our code
work in the first place?

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.500
And second of all, does
our code work well?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.360
So lower-level questions we can
ask under this heading include,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.790
but are not limited
to, does our code

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.970
work with style and
language guidelines?

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.510
Does our code work with
its latest dependencies?

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.450
Does our code work when
subjected to extreme loads?

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:24.310
Or perhaps even,
does our code work

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.010
when provided malicious or
otherwise invalid input?

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.680
So along this, there's
a broad spectrum

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.150
of tests we can
rely on to help us

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.480
answer the question of whether
or not our code works at all.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.250
So off to the
left, you'll notice

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:43.550
we have unit tests,
which, generally speaking,

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.496
are faster and more specific.

00:01:45.496 --> 00:01:47.120
Off to the right, we
have system tests,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.207
which are slower and
more comprehensive.

00:01:49.207 --> 00:01:51.290
And then in the middle,
we have integration tests,

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.510
which are kind of a
hybrid of the two.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.720
So generally speaking, the
goal of this presentation

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.790
is to get you to
a point where you

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.940
can figure out whether or
not your code works at all.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:03.950
We are less concerned
about figuring out

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.780
why it does not work,
although that is

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.500
something I will get to later.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.160
So along those
lines, system tests

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.670
are typically more essential
for determining whether or not

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.260
your code works at all.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.720
Now, unit tests can be
helpful when it comes time

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.140
to determine why your code
doesn't work because they give

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.350
you greater granularity
of what the failures are

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.730
and what things are going wrong.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:31.940
So as a rough indication of
how many types of each test

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.480
you need, on the bottom of the
slide we have a rough diagram.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.200
So imagine that
you have a feature.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.830
And this feature is
tested by one system test.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.260
So if you were to cover
that feature completely

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.530
with integration tests, you
might need two integration

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.080
tests, or perhaps even
up to four unit tests

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.030
to cover that
feature completely.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.800
Note that these
ratios are not exact,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.280
they are simply an example.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.500
So the second high-level
aspect of testing

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.430
that we can investigate is,
not only does our code work,

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:06.940
but does it work well?

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.040
So static tests
are a type of test

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.260
that tests your code
for things such as style

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.220
without actually
running the code.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:15.100
All the other tests we
will talk about today

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.580
involve actually running
a part of your code.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.700
So load testing, this
is a type of system test

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.220
that directly measures
your code's ability

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.100
to handle either expected
or unexpected loads.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.050
Basically, you throw a
bunch of traffic at it

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.170
and see if it can
handle the punishment.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.620
And then, fuzz tests and
vulnerability scanners

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.262
test your code's ability
to handle malicious input,

00:03:38.262 --> 00:03:39.970
so if people try to
hack your application

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:40.803
or things like that.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.390
So as far as why we test--

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.550
so testing can be thought
of as an insurance

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.370
policy against potential bugs
and their associated business

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.485
impact.

00:03:51.485 --> 00:03:53.610
So if you ever need to
justify to management, well,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.020
why should we write tests?

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.790
It's not so much,
they prevent bugs,

00:03:56.790 --> 00:03:58.860
as much as it is,
they prevent bugs

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.110
which might have a business
impact on our bottom line.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.260
So the first goal of
testing is to reduce

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.120
the likelihood of
software errors happening

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.990
in the first place.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:09.910
The second is to
verify code correctness

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.530
on an ongoing basis.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.830
If you wanted to manually
test your code once or twice,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:15.610
that's easy enough to do.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.610
The problem comes when
you want to test it, say,

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.320
every week or every day.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.375
After a while, the
cost spent manually

00:04:21.375 --> 00:04:24.000
testing is greater than the cost
of just automating it and then

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.430
running an automated test suite
each time you want to test it.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.280
So the stretch goal here
is to quickly identify

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.590
the errors caught by
any test that we have.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.150
It's not strictly
necessary, but it

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.660
would be helpful in
the debugging process.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.120
So as far as the
impact of testing,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.610
Google's OSS-Fuzz
fuzzing project

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.120
found more than 250 potential
security vulnerabilities

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:49.610
in major open source software
within its first five

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.070
months of execution.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:53.720
And inadequate
software testing has

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.730
been estimated to cost
the United States up

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.899
to 1% of its annual GDP alone.

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:01.690
And here are the
citations for those stats.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.340
So now that we've
talked a little bit

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.780
about testing as
a concept, we're

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.510
going to move on to applying
that to the world of functions

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:11.860
as a service.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.880
So first off as kind of
a recap about unit tests,

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.950
the core idea of a
unit test is to verify

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.650
that the minutiae, or the
individual units of your code,

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.010
work as expected, so things
like edge case checking.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:30.610
Now, in a unit test, there is
no reliance on or verification

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.100
of external dependencies,
such as libraries, APIs,

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:36.210
or even outright
cloud platforms.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.790
But to get around this,
we use mocking frameworks

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.180
to fake those
external dependencies.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.270
So in general, unit tests
are useful for investigating

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.400
known problems in things
like regressions and whatnot.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.720
However, they are not as good at
finding unanticipated problems

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.500
that come out of left field.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.067
But they are fast to run and
rarely use build resources.

00:05:59.067 --> 00:06:00.900
So typically because
they're self-contained,

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.810
you don't have to use any actual
Cloud resources to run them,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:08.940
so they're almost
cheap or free to run.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.520
And before we leave
this slide, there's

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.680
a diagram on the
bottom right hand,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.490
basically this explains how
the mocking concept works.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:19.530
So normally, you'd
have a feature.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:22.260
And that feature calls an API,
the API returns a response,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.490
and the feature does
something with that response.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:27.180
The mocking framework
allows you to completely cut

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.030
the API out of the
conversation and say,

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.150
I'm going to return a predefined
response to the feature,

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.730
and I'm going to assert that
the feature behaves in the way

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.970
that I think it should if
the API had actually returned

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.290
that response to the feature.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.860
That's the core idea
of unit testing.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:48.680
So here, we have a sample
HTTP Google Cloud Function.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.390
This is a pretty
straightforward function

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.450
that essentially
creates a bucket

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.580
when given a name parameter
through a POST request.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.760
So it will take
this POST request.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.860
It will attempt to create
the bucket on line 4,

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.230
using the
storage.createBucket function.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.932
If that process fails, it
will send back an error

00:07:05.932 --> 00:07:06.890
with the error message.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.056
If it succeeds, it will
send back a success message.

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:15.580
So here's a sample
HTTP unit test.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:17.410
You'll notice that
on line 1, we import

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.310
a bunch of different libraries.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.550
So walking through
them, proxyquire

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.330
is a really interesting
library that, in Node.js, lets

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:32.350
us sort of hook into, or mock,
a client library, or rather,

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.240
a Node client library.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.330
So in this case, we have this
google-cloud/storage client

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.200
library.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.080
And we're replacing it,
we're hooking it over,

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:46.000
with a gcsMock object,
which we define on line 2.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.250
Now, that gcsMock object is a
sort of subset of that library.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.770
And we define a
createBucket function there

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:57.970
that we want to receive any
calls to storage.createBucket

00:07:57.970 --> 00:07:59.540
in our actual code.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:02.410
So instead of saying
something like,

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:04.750
we're going to actually
import the real library,

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.620
we import this
fake version of it,

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:09.640
this mock version of it
that we declared on line 2.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:13.750
So once we do that, we can rely
on the library called sinon,

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:15.550
which gives us lots
of useful helpers

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.330
to log what kinds of arguments
are being passed to functions.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.760
So you'll notice line 2,
we declare something called

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.100
a stub, so sinon.stub().

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:26.650
And the thing
about that is, that

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:28.690
will log any arguments
that are passed to it.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.520
It will log how many times
it's called, things like that.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.400
And then, we can inspect that
stub to figure out, OK, well,

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.640
how did the program
that we passed this stub

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:39.679
to actually use that stub?

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:42.940
So then, we move down
to our test on line 6.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.310
Notice that here, we
attempt to create a bucket,

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:47.740
we do not actually
create a bucket.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.780
So on line 7, we
declare our bucket name.

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:53.050
Lines 8 and 9, we
declare our mock versions

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.270
of request and
response parameters.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:59.516
If you've worked with
Cloud Functions before

00:08:59.516 --> 00:09:00.890
or have seen the
code previously,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.490
you'll notice that our function
took those parameters, request

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:04.390
and response.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.850
So we have to
mock, or fake, them

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.260
and pass them into our program.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:15.500
So once we do that, we then
run our original function

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:19.090
on line 10 using the
program.createBucket call

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:19.840
there.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.510
And we pass that fake
request and response object

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.319
into our program
just as if it had

00:09:24.319 --> 00:09:26.860
been running on Cloud Functions
and received the real version

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:28.360
of those objects.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:31.630
And then, on line 11, we check
the createBucket function,

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.540
or rather, our fake
createBucket function

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:37.195
with the correct argument, in
this case, the name constant.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.500
So moving on to
integration tests,

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.430
the core idea here is to
verify that the various parts

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.450
of your code fit
together as expected.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:52.420
So integration tests,
broadly speaking,

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:54.820
can either use mocking
or rely directly

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:57.320
on external dependencies.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.830
Now, external dependencies
that they do rely on

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:03.250
are usually fast
and/or cheap to access,

00:10:03.250 --> 00:10:05.860
thus keeping the individual
test runs moderately

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.110
fast, moderately quick.

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:10.720
But note that if you're
depending on actually billed

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.359
resources, you would have to
pay for those invocations,

00:10:13.359 --> 00:10:14.900
you would have to
pay for those runs.

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.020
So this may require
small amounts of money

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.350
to run each iteration
of your tests.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:23.530
But generally speaking, they
balance problem, detection,

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:24.730
and isolation.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.160
And this is the cool part
about integration tests

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:30.460
is that in some cases, they
can detect unanticipated bugs.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.010
Whereas unit tests can only
detect things that you really

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:34.932
specifically check
for, integration tests

00:10:34.932 --> 00:10:36.640
have a large enough
scope that if there's

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.796
some bug that happens that you
haven't thought to check for,

00:10:39.796 --> 00:10:41.920
there's a better probability
that integration tests

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.760
will catch it.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:48.317
And just as a rough sort
of visual analog here,

00:10:48.317 --> 00:10:49.900
where an integration
test comes in is,

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:51.670
let's say you have
two or more features,

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:53.920
an integration test
tests their ability

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.390
to sort of operate
between each other.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:57.970
So if you have
three features, you

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:00.520
want to see how well they
talk to each other, that's

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.145
where an integration
test will come in.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.950
And here's a sample
HTTP integration test.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:11.080
You'll notice again, we
import a bunch of libraries

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:13.960
here, some of which are the
same, some of which are not.

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.840
Ava is our testing
harness, so that's just

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.600
a testing framework in Node.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:22.240
UUID is a universal
unique identifier library,

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.740
I'll get to why that's
important later.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:26.680
And then
request-promise-native is

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:30.760
a library that allows us to
make HTTP requests in Node.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:36.270
So notice on line 2, we have
a standard Node require call.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.880
So we are importing a live
version of the Google Cloud

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.319
Storage library.

00:11:40.319 --> 00:11:42.360
This library, we are not
mocking this dependency,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.940
we are importing it as-is.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:49.980
Now, on line 4, we attempt
to create a real bucket.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:53.660
And we do this by first
declaring a bunch of values.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:56.540
In this case, we have to
declare a name for the bucket.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.310
Note that we use
that UUID library

00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.900
that I mentioned
earlier, because we

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:02.690
want to ensure that our bucket
names are unique whenever we're

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:05.152
trying to create any
actual Cloud resources.

00:12:05.152 --> 00:12:06.860
If we don't, it's
possible that we end up

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.504
into some race condition or
something like that where

00:12:09.504 --> 00:12:11.420
we try to create a bucket
that already exists,

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.211
or we try to delete
one that doesn't exist,

00:12:13.211 --> 00:12:15.240
things like that.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.600
So to avoid that, we use
that UUID function there.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:22.250
Now, once we do that, we can
declare the URL of the function

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:24.950
we want to attempt to
send this request to.

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:26.960
In this case, it's
going to be a local URL.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:29.335
So this BASE_URL
environment variable

00:12:29.335 --> 00:12:30.710
is probably going
to be something

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:32.930
like localhost or whatever.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:36.500
And then, on line 7, we
execute that HTTP request.

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:38.690
We say, OK, here's
our target URL,

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:41.060
let's pass the name to
it, so on and so forth.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.970
Note that even when
that request returns,

00:12:43.970 --> 00:12:47.310
the bucket itself may not
have yet been created.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:50.540
So to avoid that, we have
to kind of either use

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:54.050
exponential back-off, which the
idea of exponential back-off

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:55.645
is, you wait a certain
amount of time,

00:12:55.645 --> 00:12:57.020
then you wait that
amount of time

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:59.780
times 2, that amount of time
times 4, so on and so forth,

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.970
constantly multiplying
by some constant,

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:03.960
or perhaps even adding
some random jitter

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:06.290
so that if you're doing a
bunch of exponential back-off

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:10.500
operations, they don't
sort of all fall together.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.250
But in this case, it's
easy and simple enough

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:14.700
to implement a constant delay.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:17.210
And that's exactly
what we do on line 8.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:20.180
So we say, we're going to wait
one second between requesting

00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.970
that the bucket be created and
actually checking if it exists,

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:25.820
because the bucket creation
API is eventually consistent,

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:27.840
like a lot of Cloud APIs.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:32.780
So we do that on line 10, we
check that the bucket exists.

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:34.580
And if it does, then
our test passes.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:36.204
If it doesn't, then,
well, our function

00:13:36.204 --> 00:13:38.710
didn't do what we wanted
to do, and our test fails.

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:44.020
So moving on to system tests--

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:46.600
and kind of as a recap, note
that system tests are all

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:49.369
about verifying your
code as a system.

00:13:49.369 --> 00:13:51.910
There's a heavy reliance here
on external dependencies, which

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.130
can slow down the test itself.

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:56.950
And sometimes, system tests
will require moderate amounts

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:59.140
of build resources, because
you're directing traffic

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.980
at an actual cloud instance.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:05.410
Now, system tests have
pretty good bug detection,

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:08.650
even if bugs are unexpected
or outside your codebase.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:11.831
This is where they really
sort of show their true power.

00:14:11.831 --> 00:14:13.330
If you're trying
to figure out, do I

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:16.450
have a bug in my dependencies,
or is there a bug, perhaps,

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:18.640
in some configuration
in my Cloud Platform?

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:21.640
That's where system tests will
hunt for that sort of thing.

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:24.130
Integration tests might
find, unit tests probably

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:26.350
won't find it, but system
tests have a good chance

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:29.080
of finding those types of bugs.

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.960
But note that they are bad at
isolating problems, generally

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.660
speaking, and their root causes,
just because system tests have

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:38.084
such a large scope
that they test.

00:14:38.084 --> 00:14:39.750
One thing to take
away, and we discussed

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:42.510
this in the integration
tests sample as well,

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.370
is that state matters.

00:14:44.370 --> 00:14:46.980
And it may introduce
either eventual consistency

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:49.930
and/or shared resource issues.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.105
And what I mean by shared
resource issues is,

00:14:52.105 --> 00:14:54.480
if you're trying to create a
resource that already exists

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.910
or you're trying to delete a
resource that doesn't exist,

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:59.076
say maybe you're running
two tests at the same time,

00:14:59.076 --> 00:15:01.680
and they both create and
delete the same test bucket,

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:04.950
then it's possible that that
will introduce race conditions

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.871
or things like that that make
your test results essentially

00:15:07.871 --> 00:15:08.370
flaky.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:15.140
So here's a sample
HTTP system test.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:17.330
Some of you might notice
that this looks familiar.

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:21.350
In fact, it's exactly the
same as the integration

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:22.760
test we just discussed.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:26.360
The only difference is the
base URL that we specify.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:28.640
So instead of being something
on our local machine,

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.490
or on, perhaps, a local
machine on the network,

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:35.210
it's going to be the actual
deployed Cloud Function URL.

00:15:35.210 --> 00:15:37.880
So it's going to be
something like US, Central

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:42.430
1, some region, dash your
project ID @cloudfunctions.net.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:47.230
Now, the thing to
bear in mind here

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:51.280
is the challenges of
those unique test runs,

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:57.130
that state dependence, and the
asynchronous nature of creating

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:59.180
that bucket still apply.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:02.259
So those are more common
in terms of system tests.

00:16:02.259 --> 00:16:04.050
Sometimes they happen
in integration tests,

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.074
sometimes you can mock
those dependencies out.

00:16:06.074 --> 00:16:07.990
But in system tests,
they're almost guaranteed

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:10.980
to happen if you're working
with a Cloud platform.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:14.050
And the one caveat that I want
to make sure I get to today

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:17.560
is this system test being the
same as the integration test,

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.000
this is a trope
you can typically

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.790
exploit when you're dealing
with HTTP triggered functions.

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:24.165
If you're dealing with something
like a PUB/SUB triggered

00:16:24.165 --> 00:16:26.170
function, or a Firebase
triggered function,

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:29.116
or something like that, it's
a lot harder to just do this

00:16:29.116 --> 00:16:31.240
and simply say, my integration
test and system test

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:34.780
are going to be identical.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:38.160
So moving on to static
testing, so as a recap,

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:41.100
the goal of static testing is to
verify that your code follows,

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.280
typically, either language and
style conventions or dependency

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.680
management best practices.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:48.660
Now, many of these
static testing tools

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.790
are free to install
and quick to use.

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.510
So there are two types
of static testing tools

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.890
that I want to make sure
we talk about today.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.440
The first of which is linters.

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.540
And linters are really
useful because they enforce

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:02.691
style conventions on your code.

00:17:02.691 --> 00:17:04.440
And sometimes, they
can also detect things

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.300
like interpretation
or compilation errors.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.480
So for example,
for Node, there's

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.824
a linter tool called Prettier.

00:17:11.824 --> 00:17:13.740
There's also a couple
of different other tools

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:16.281
like ESLint or Semi-Standard
that you might have heard of.

00:17:16.281 --> 00:17:18.030
If you're a Python
developer, you probably

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:22.710
know about Pylint or Flake8, or
Pep8, or something like that.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:24.569
And the dependency
management tools

00:17:24.569 --> 00:17:27.150
essentially check for issues
with your dependencies.

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:30.510
So in Node, we have this tool
called Snyk, which effectively

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:33.150
checks for known insecure
versions of our dependencies

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:34.860
anywhere in our dependency tree.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:36.630
So let's say you depend
on a package that

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:38.580
depends on the package
that is insecure,

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.490
Snyk will detect that and send
you an alert if you've set it

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:44.340
up to do so.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.920
So the major con
of static testing

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:47.720
is that they have a
very narrow focus.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.210
They're very good at
doing what they do,

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:54.680
but they only do a very
small amount of things.

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:58.000
And as an example of
why linting is useful,

00:17:58.000 --> 00:17:59.560
you'll notice the
code on the left

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:03.220
is basically some JavaScript,
some contrived JavaScript,

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:04.710
but it's prior to linting.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.970
And on the right,
we have that code

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.970
after we've fix the
appropriate linting errors.

00:18:09.970 --> 00:18:12.724
So on the left, basically,
the big issue here is--

00:18:12.724 --> 00:18:14.140
if you're not
immediately familiar

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:15.556
with the details
of JavaScript, it

00:18:15.556 --> 00:18:19.360
might not be obvious
what the value of a is.

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:23.050
Now, someone who is, like
even a person like me who's

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:25.790
worked with JavaScript for
quite awhile might think, oh, a

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:27.520
is null, just
looking at that code.

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:30.310
But in fact, when you run
it, I found out, even,

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:32.210
that a is undefined.

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:35.920
So declaring it on a
separate line on the right

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.260
makes it easier for other
programmers to understand.

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:41.110
Now, the other thing
that the linter

00:18:41.110 --> 00:18:44.530
flagged in our left
sample is that we

00:18:44.530 --> 00:18:46.400
have a double equal sign there.

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:49.300
Now, in JavaScript, a double
equal sign essentially means,

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.490
can I cast a to b?

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:54.020
Not necessarily are they the
same type and the same value,

00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:57.280
it's more, are they the same
value and can they be casted?

00:18:57.280 --> 00:18:59.675
So we go off to the
right, we fix that,

00:18:59.675 --> 00:19:01.300
because the linter
says, did you really

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:03.610
mean to just check
value equality,

00:19:03.610 --> 00:19:06.410
or do you mean to check
type equality as well?

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:09.830
And in this situation, we
meant to check type equality,

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:11.530
so we add a third equal sign.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:14.850
And then, we get that
undefined and null.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:17.530
They might be the same value,
but they are not the same type.

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:19.030
And therefore,
they are not equal.

00:19:19.030 --> 00:19:21.880
And you'll notice on the
left, that console.log

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:23.380
statement returns true.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:24.980
On the right, it returns false.

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:27.170
So this can have an
impact in your code.

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:33.060
So moving on to the
idea of load testing,

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:36.540
so the idea of load testing is
to verify your entire system

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:38.880
from end to end,
including, importantly,

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:44.370
the non-autoscaled components'
ability to handle user demand.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:48.480
So here, there is heavy reliance
on external dependencies.

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.360
And these dependencies may be
slow to access, which can slow

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:54.060
down the test runs themselves.

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:56.160
Note that a lot of
load testing tools

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:59.220
out there are typically free,
or at least open source.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.320
But the test runs
themselves can require

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:04.110
lots of build resources, because
essentially what you're doing

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.550
is creating vast
amounts of traffic

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:08.190
and then directing
it at your app.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:12.350
And of course, you have to
pay to process that traffic.

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:14.530
But load tests,
generally speaking,

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.080
help protect against sudden,
and potentially beneficial,

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:18.352
traffic spikes.

00:20:18.352 --> 00:20:19.810
The example I like
to give is like,

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:21.622
especially if you're
in retail, there

00:20:21.622 --> 00:20:23.830
are certain seasons of the
year, like here in the US,

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:27.040
it's probably like Black Friday,
where retail just suddenly

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:28.990
has a glut of customers.

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:32.020
Everyone is looking to buy
your product all of a sudden.

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:34.720
Or perhaps if you work
at a small startup,

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:38.050
and suddenly your
product gets featured

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:40.660
on a major internet news
site or a major newspaper,

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:44.860
and your user base increases
by a factor of three, or four,

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:45.670
or five.

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:47.425
So how do you test that?

00:20:51.340 --> 00:20:53.590
If we make it big,
so to speak, how

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.260
are we going to make sure that
our site can handle that load?

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.676
Well, that's where you rely
on something like a load test.

00:20:58.676 --> 00:21:01.465
And again, those traffic
spikes can be beneficial.

00:21:01.465 --> 00:21:03.140
It need not be
like a distributed

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:07.210
denial of service or someone
trying to take down your app.

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:09.805
And some examples of
load testing tools--

00:21:09.805 --> 00:21:11.430
we have Apache Bench,
which we're going

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.030
to demo on the next slide.

00:21:13.030 --> 00:21:16.060
And that is preinstalled on
most Mac and Linux systems,

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:19.820
so you can just type AB on
a terminal, and it's there.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:21.850
The other option is
to use Apache JMeter

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:24.810
if you want something
that is more configurable.

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:29.010
So without further ado,
we'll talk about the demo.

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:32.730
And you'll notice here, we
have a standard terminal.

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:34.510
Can anybody not see this?

00:21:34.510 --> 00:21:37.478
Raise your hand if
you cannot see this.

00:21:37.478 --> 00:21:38.059
Good.

00:21:38.059 --> 00:21:40.100
So what we're going to
do-- and for good measure,

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:41.350
I'll make it a little larger--

00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:46.850
so we're going to
run a command here

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:49.950
that does a load test for us.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.640
So here, we're
running Apache Bench,

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:54.950
and notice what we're
doing here is, we're

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:57.870
running 50 requests total.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.950
So we're running 50 requests
total with up to 10 requests

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:04.860
at once, so concurrency
of 10, to this link,

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:06.680
which is just a
simple HTTP Cloud

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.360
Function that I've set up.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:12.210
And importantly, if we take
a look at the stats here,

00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:15.150
we notice that all of
our requests succeeded

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:18.060
and none of our requests failed.

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:21.240
So that means that we can
count on this function

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:25.284
to handle the amount of load
that we've tested it to.

00:22:25.284 --> 00:22:27.450
There's also lots of other
sort of interesting stats

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:30.450
here, like you can see
request percentiles,

00:22:30.450 --> 00:22:34.020
the median request here
took 81 seconds to complete.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.690
The longest request
took maybe 6 seconds,

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:38.910
which is probably due to
something like a cold start

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:41.680
or something like that.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:45.194
And before we get going, I'm
going to execute the next step,

00:22:45.194 --> 00:22:46.360
and we'll get to that later.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:52.550
So moving on to
security testing--

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:54.560
so to recap, the goal
of security testing

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:57.620
is to verify your
code's ability to handle

00:22:57.620 --> 00:22:59.810
malicious input, and
that of your dependencies

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:01.730
as well, equally importantly.

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:03.560
So the motivation
for security testing

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:07.760
is that US business has
lost $67 billion, with a B,

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:10.490
to cyber attacks in 2005.

00:23:10.490 --> 00:23:13.750
That was more than 10 years ago.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:16.610
So security tests can occur
anywhere in your testing stack.

00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:19.370
They can be part of unit tests,
system tests, integration

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:22.040
tests, and or static tests.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:25.500
Now, security testing tools
are sometimes free to install,

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:26.750
they're often free to install.

00:23:26.750 --> 00:23:29.210
There are some you can purchase.

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:32.420
But they may cost money if
build resources are involved.

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.850
Like if you're testing your
actual Cloud deployment

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:36.950
and you have to pay
for that traffic,

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:39.280
they send potentially
malicious traffic to your Cloud

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:42.790
deployment, you have to
pay for that traffic.

00:23:42.790 --> 00:23:45.190
So some examples of
security testing tools--

00:23:45.190 --> 00:23:48.022
there's the Open Web
Application Security Project,

00:23:48.022 --> 00:23:49.480
which is a great
resource if you're

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:52.480
working on web applications.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:56.230
And they have something called
the Zed Attack Proxy, or ZAP,

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:57.806
and that is an
automated testing tool

00:23:57.806 --> 00:23:59.680
that will go through
your website and attempt

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:03.680
to find security
vulnerabilities in your app.

00:24:03.680 --> 00:24:05.740
So then, there's Snyk,
which I mentioned earlier.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.350
It checks for known
insecure dependencies.

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:10.987
The Big List of Naughty
Strings, essentially it's

00:24:10.987 --> 00:24:13.320
a list of strings that could
potentially break your app,

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:16.180
so things like SQL
injection, or Unicode errors,

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:18.350
or things like that.

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:20.380
And then, you have
the OSS-Fuzz tool

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:24.030
that I mentioned earlier,
which is written by Google.

00:24:24.030 --> 00:24:27.430
And a couple of caveats to bear
in mind with security testing--

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:29.760
if you take away nothing
from the slide, this

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:33.450
is probably the most
important thing to take away.

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:37.570
So first of all, no
automated tool is perfect.

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:40.980
And if you're serious about
security, hire a consultant.

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:42.630
They will get you
closer to perfection,

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:45.420
but they probably won't
get you all the way.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:48.180
And then, these
security testing tools

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:50.010
may actually damage
the environment

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.180
that they are pointed at.

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:53.320
Some tools are better
about this than others.

00:24:53.320 --> 00:24:58.426
But if a tool, say,
tries to drop a database,

00:24:58.426 --> 00:25:00.300
and you point it at your
production instance,

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:02.220
and it is successful,
then guess what,

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:04.120
you've dropped your
production database.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:06.510
So when you're dealing with
security testing tools,

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:11.160
make sure to test them in a
test environment or something

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:14.760
where you're either 100%
confident that it is totally

00:25:14.760 --> 00:25:17.070
secure, at least from that
particular security testing

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:18.000
tool.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:21.719
Or if it gets blown
up, who cares?

00:25:21.719 --> 00:25:23.760
Maybe you lose some test
data, but you can either

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:24.996
restore from a backup or--

00:25:24.996 --> 00:25:27.120
it's the type of thing your
customers won't notice,

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:28.078
let me put it that way.

00:25:30.407 --> 00:25:32.740
And here's a citation for
that stat I mentioned earlier.

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:38.910
So moving on to the
sort of CI/CD pipeline

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:43.060
and talking about how to
automate this testing process--

00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:45.840
so the core idea here is
that for many developers,

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:48.100
git branches are
the source of truth.

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:49.740
Now, if you don't
use git, you probably

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:52.140
use some version control
system, whether that's

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:56.350
SVN, or CVS, or Mercurial,
or something like that.

00:25:56.350 --> 00:25:59.460
But the core idea is, you think
in terms of a version control

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:00.900
system.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:04.680
And normally, function code
must be tested and redeployed

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:05.349
manually.

00:26:05.349 --> 00:26:07.140
Like in the Google
Cloud Functions context,

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:09.810
you have to do your
GCloud Functions deploy,

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:14.610
or you have to deploy it
through the UI console.

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:18.150
So to get around this, we can
use continuous integration

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.670
and continuous deployment
systems, or pipelines,

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:23.260
to automate that step.

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:26.430
Now, there are many different
CI/CD options available.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.360
In the context of
this presentation,

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:30.551
we will use Cloud
Build, primarily

00:26:30.551 --> 00:26:32.550
because it natively
integrates with Google Cloud

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:35.700
Platform and stores
repositories as well,

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:38.970
in our case, GitHub
and Bitbucket.

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:44.160
So it's actually pretty
easy to set up a basic CI/CD

00:26:44.160 --> 00:26:45.257
for Cloud Functions.

00:26:45.257 --> 00:26:47.590
It does take some time, it
does take some configuration.

00:26:47.590 --> 00:26:50.800
But you could probably do
it in about half a day.

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:58.080
So here's an example build
configuration for Node.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.510
This file is the
heart of Cloud Build.

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:02.610
This essentially
tells Cloud Build

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:07.977
what it needs to do to run your
tests and deploy your code.

00:27:07.977 --> 00:27:10.560
So if you're a Node developer,
this will look pretty familiar.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:12.909
If you're not, then we
can walk through it.

00:27:12.909 --> 00:27:15.450
But essentially, what's going
on here with the first step is,

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:17.290
we're installing
our dependencies.

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:19.290
If you've worked with
Python, this will probably

00:27:19.290 --> 00:27:21.510
look like PIP install to you.

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:23.880
Now, the second test
is running our tests.

00:27:23.880 --> 00:27:25.560
If you're a Python
developer, this

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:29.170
will look like PyTest, or
Nox, or something like that.

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:32.430
But if you're a Node
developer, you use npm-test.

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:35.290
And the third, this
is an optional step,

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:39.990
but if you don't want any
sort of old zombie function

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:42.540
instances from your old
function lying around,

00:27:42.540 --> 00:27:44.080
you have to go delete it.

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:46.890
So you can do that by doing
the GCloud functions delete

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:47.880
command.

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:51.240
Now, if you would rather have
those zombie instances around

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:53.190
and not risk
potentially deleting

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:55.230
and not being able to
deploy your function

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:57.780
through an automated
test, it's probably better

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:00.540
just to deal with the
zombie instances and just

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:01.820
nix this step.

00:28:01.820 --> 00:28:03.930
But if you care more
about zombie instances

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:07.470
than potential failures
on the very last step,

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:11.070
then you probably want to
include a delete command just

00:28:11.070 --> 00:28:13.279
to get rid of those
zombie instances.

00:28:13.279 --> 00:28:14.820
And then, of course,
our last command

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:17.439
deploys our actual
function code.

00:28:17.439 --> 00:28:19.480
And it's just a standard
GCloud functions deploy,

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:21.130
so on and so forth.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:23.910
Now, the thing to bear
in mind is this executes

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:25.360
from the top down.

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:28.680
So if any of these steps
fail, anything below them

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:30.220
will not execute.

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:34.350
So if your tests fail, you
will not delete or deploy

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.070
the new version
of your function.

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:38.460
Now, if the deletion
succeeds, but the deployment

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:40.710
fails, like I said
earlier, you will end up

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.462
without a function deployed.

00:28:42.462 --> 00:28:43.920
And if that is bad
for you, if that

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:46.530
is worse than having some
old function instances lying

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:51.840
around, we recommend
nixing that third step.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:55.160
So now, we're going to move
to a demo of Cloud Build.

00:28:55.160 --> 00:28:58.550
But if we move to Cloud Build
here, what we're going to do

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:01.680
is, we're going to make
a change to this repo.

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:05.120
So we're going to
execute it now.

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:07.790
Let's first take a look
at the commit logs here.

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:10.220
So we notice that we have
this commit here that says,

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:12.320
we're going to
change hello to hi.

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:14.549
And maybe our manager
comes to us and says,

00:29:14.549 --> 00:29:15.590
I don't like this commit.

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:16.673
You need to get rid of it.

00:29:16.673 --> 00:29:17.840
Remove it.

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:20.770
So we can do that using a--

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:25.750
let me check the log again,
make sure I copy the commit--

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:33.410
git reset, that commit, check
our status, check our log,

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:34.490
git reset.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:39.010
Let's try this--
soft, there we go.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:39.930
Whoops, that failed.

00:29:39.930 --> 00:29:41.096
So let's try something else.

00:29:43.294 --> 00:29:44.960
When all else fails,
nuke it from orbit.

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:52.912
And then, we can push
this to our master.

00:29:52.912 --> 00:29:54.370
Maybe this is a
prod release, so we

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:56.245
want to make sure that
we just push it as-is.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.512
It's pushing now.

00:30:05.512 --> 00:30:07.220
And if we take a look
at the last commit,

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:11.000
we'll notice it's this
commit hash right here.

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:16.990
And we can go to our lovely
Container Builder, refresh it,

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:19.870
and we notice that we've
kicked off a build right here.

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:22.840
So if we go there,
we notice this

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:27.040
is in response to that same
git commit we just pushed.

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:29.560
So we can scroll down,
and we get a list of these

00:30:29.560 --> 00:30:31.120
build steps here
as we defined them

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:33.680
in that cloudbuild.yaml file.

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:35.920
So first, we're installing
our dependencies.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:37.510
Then, we're running our tests.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:39.801
Then, we're deleting our old
versions of the functions,

00:30:39.801 --> 00:30:41.890
notice the beta here,
because this command is old.

00:30:41.890 --> 00:30:46.044
And then, we are deploying
our version of the function.

00:30:46.044 --> 00:30:47.710
Now, if we scroll
down to our logs here,

00:30:47.710 --> 00:30:49.251
we notice that the
build is starting.

00:30:49.251 --> 00:30:53.050
It'll take a second or
two to kind of get going.

00:30:53.050 --> 00:30:55.680
I'm going to zoom out a little
bit here so we can scroll here.

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:58.170
So it's installing
our dependencies here,

00:30:58.170 --> 00:31:01.020
it's executing step 0.

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:04.620
These are just sort of
NPM prints, and NPM logs.

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:09.480
Then, we are running our tests
using npm run integration-test.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:14.300
And then, we're starting
our quasi-emulator or shim

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:15.770
and trying to run our tests.

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:18.640
We notice our tests
are passing, 2 passed,

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:21.860
2 passed, scrolling
down, so all of our tests

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:24.437
are starting to pass here.

00:31:24.437 --> 00:31:25.770
So all of our tests have passed.

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:29.140
Woo-hoo, we can now delete our
old version of the function,

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.970
and we can deploy it.

00:31:30.970 --> 00:31:36.300
So as far as deleting it goes,
that's what we're doing here.

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:38.060
And it's running that
deletion operation

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:41.710
to delete the old
version of the function.

00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:44.300
And then, we are deploying
the function here.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.740
So we've already
deployed the function.

00:31:46.740 --> 00:31:48.690
And the deployment has finished.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:50.610
Now, if we go to this
URL, we'll notice

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:55.650
that before we had done this
commit, if I make this larger,

00:31:55.650 --> 00:31:58.180
this used to say, "Hi World!"

00:31:58.180 --> 00:32:00.790
And our manager came to us
and said, I don't want that.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:02.440
I want it to say "Hello World!"

00:32:02.440 --> 00:32:06.314
But if we refresh it now,
it says, "Hello World!"

00:32:06.314 --> 00:32:07.480
as our manager asked us for.

00:32:13.902 --> 00:32:15.860
So now that we've talked
a little bit about how

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:19.160
to automate that
testing process,

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:23.450
we can talk about what to
do when, the heavens forbid,

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:28.090
something goes wrong and a
bug ends up in production.

00:32:28.090 --> 00:32:30.390
So GCF ops 101--

00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:33.750
basically, the core idea here
is that tests can only detect

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:37.000
problems out of production.

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:38.790
So for problems in
production, we're

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:40.660
going to need to do
something different.

00:32:40.660 --> 00:32:42.740
And that something
different is Stackdriver.

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:45.930
So Stackdriver is a set of tools
for monitoring and debugging

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:48.040
code on GCP.

00:32:48.040 --> 00:32:50.350
Now, the most useful
tools for Cloud Functions

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:53.470
in my humble opinion are,
first of all, monitoring

00:32:53.470 --> 00:32:56.020
and metrics, which help us
figure out whether or not

00:32:56.020 --> 00:32:59.980
our code is broken at all, and
logging and error reporting,

00:32:59.980 --> 00:33:05.140
which help us figure out where
and why our code is broken.

00:33:05.140 --> 00:33:07.330
So for example, with
logging and error reporting,

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:09.190
these are pretty
straightforward.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:11.260
Logging is kind of
a plug-and-play,

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:14.080
it stores and indexes
logs from your function.

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:16.210
Error reporting is also
fairly plug-and-play.

00:33:16.210 --> 00:33:18.250
It's based off of
logs and it aggregates

00:33:18.250 --> 00:33:19.990
logs into meaningful
error reports,

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:21.650
as you can see on the right.

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:24.070
So I know that the font
might be a little bit

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:26.650
small, so I'll try to narrate
through what's going on here.

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:29.530
On the left, we have a sample
log entry, which essentially

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:31.570
says, there is an error--

00:33:31.570 --> 00:33:34.200
uninitialized email address.

00:33:34.200 --> 00:33:38.620
Now, this is in some
Cloud Function called,

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:41.410
as we'll see on the right,
it's called onNewMessage.

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:44.331
So on the right, we have
stats about how frequently

00:33:44.331 --> 00:33:45.330
this error has occurred.

00:33:45.330 --> 00:33:48.520
It was first seen 13 days ago,
it was last seen 6 days ago,

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:50.620
it's occurred 152
times, we have a sample

00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:52.240
stack trace and a histogram.

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:53.860
So this is a useful
command center

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:56.590
for triaging any errors
that you might have.

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:01.590
Now, as far as monitoring
and metrics go,

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:03.330
they are not as plug-and-play.

00:34:03.330 --> 00:34:05.490
But they are just as,
if not more, useful

00:34:05.490 --> 00:34:07.920
than logging and
error reporting.

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:10.199
So generally speaking,
Stackdriver's monitoring

00:34:10.199 --> 00:34:13.409
and metrics tools help us
detect production bugs quickly.

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:15.150
They will not
necessarily guarantee

00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:16.710
that you will detect
production bugs,

00:34:16.710 --> 00:34:18.918
but they will increase your
chances of detecting them

00:34:18.918 --> 00:34:21.090
in a timely manner.

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:22.739
So our goal with
this configuration

00:34:22.739 --> 00:34:26.489
is to catch as many errors as
we can with the least setup

00:34:26.489 --> 00:34:28.420
possible.

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:31.780
So the pros of the configuration
that we're about to talk about

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:34.659
are that it's easy to
set up, it determines

00:34:34.659 --> 00:34:39.489
if production is broken, and
it can test any function type.

00:34:39.489 --> 00:34:42.280
So the cons are that it depends
on logging statement coverage,

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:45.250
so basically any time
there's an error,

00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:47.620
you need to make sure
you're logging it.

00:34:47.620 --> 00:34:49.719
And it's not always
helpful for identifying

00:34:49.719 --> 00:34:51.790
the root causes of
why things go wrong

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:55.489
or even where things go wrong.

00:34:55.489 --> 00:34:58.070
So the sort of decent
configuration that you can use,

00:34:58.070 --> 00:35:00.080
this is like the
first line of defense,

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:02.360
if you will, against
bugs in prod,

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:05.810
is to send out an
alert if severe log

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:08.390
entries, for example,
errors, are too frequent.

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:10.490
Maybe more than
1% of invocations

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:14.540
are more than some constant
or something like that.

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:17.540
The better option, if you
have the time and sort

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:20.360
of the engineer bandwidth,
is to send out an alert

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:24.320
if function execution time and
count exceed normal limits.

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:26.330
And note that when I
say better, I recommend

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:27.510
employing both of these.

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:31.490
There's no harm in having
additional metrics.

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:33.270
If you have to pick
one or the other,

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.780
it's probably better if
you go with the first one

00:35:35.780 --> 00:35:36.860
than the second one.

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:40.830
But having both is ideal.

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:43.080
And some additional
checks you can do--

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:45.940
if you're working primarily
with HTTP-triggered functions

00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:48.540
or, likewise,
HTTPS-triggered functions,

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:51.450
you can just use
Stackdriver's uptime checks

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:54.150
to check those URLs and make
sure those functions are

00:35:54.150 --> 00:35:58.020
responding with
the correct status

00:35:58.020 --> 00:36:05.042
code, so things like 200, or
302, or so on and so forth.

00:36:05.042 --> 00:36:06.500
So without further
ado, we're going

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.900
to do a quick demo of
Stackdriver monitoring.

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:12.650
And notice, I kicked off
this command earlier.

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:22.480
Oops, it's not showing up.

00:36:22.480 --> 00:36:24.570
But I kicked off
a command earlier

00:36:24.570 --> 00:36:27.000
that was an Apache
Bench command that

00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:30.240
sent 10,000 requests
to that Cloud Function

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:31.800
that I specified earlier.

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:35.220
Now, so if we look at
our Stackdriver page,

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:37.270
which is here--

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:38.460
and then we'll refresh it.

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:46.820
So we notice that there was
a resolved incident that

00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:50.570
was declared today
at about 10:55 AM.

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:51.860
So this was an alert we got.

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:59.820
If we look at the
actual inbox here,

00:36:59.820 --> 00:37:01.860
we got a Stackdriver alert.

00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:04.440
And this was sent to
us at 10:55 today.

00:37:04.440 --> 00:37:07.080
So it's telling us that our
metric threshold for this Cloud

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:12.810
Function was violated, and
it's above our threshold of 5

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:15.690
with a value of 37.383.

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:19.890
So what this is telling us
is, you said, send me an alert

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:23.750
if more than 5 executions
were happening concurrently.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:25.260
We had 37.

00:37:25.260 --> 00:37:27.690
So if you got
something like this,

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:29.640
you'd probably want to
say, OK, well, what's

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:30.931
going wrong with this function?

00:37:30.931 --> 00:37:33.090
Is it just suddenly
super popular?

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:34.410
It some retry loop?

00:37:34.410 --> 00:37:38.130
Is some API calling
it way too many times?

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:39.176
What's going on here?

00:37:39.176 --> 00:37:40.800
And of course, once
you get this email,

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:43.367
then you can follow up and
investigate as appropriate.

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:52.070
So now that we've talked
about all this stuff,

00:37:52.070 --> 00:37:55.850
we can kind of recap
what we've learned today.

00:37:55.850 --> 00:38:00.320
So as kind of a background of
how all this works, or rather,

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:03.370
we've given a background
of how all this works

00:38:03.370 --> 00:38:07.760
and talked about an overview of
testing strategies in general.

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:10.850
Then, we talked a little
bit about applying that

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:12.530
to the world of serverless.

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:15.260
Finally, we talked
about CI/CD pipelines,

00:38:15.260 --> 00:38:18.410
how to automate this
testing on an ongoing basis.

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:19.970
And then, a little
bit about what

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:22.550
to do when, despite
our best efforts,

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:26.439
a bug gets out of our canary or
whatever and into production,

00:38:26.439 --> 00:38:27.980
and we need to do
something to detect

00:38:27.980 --> 00:38:31.400
that bug quickly and resolve
it, ideally before our customers

00:38:31.400 --> 00:38:34.010
notice.

00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:36.500
So the key points that I
would like people to take away

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:38.875
from this presentation-- if
there's a side where you want

00:38:38.875 --> 00:38:42.050
to take a photo, this is it.

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:44.780
So narrating through
this, basically testing

00:38:44.780 --> 00:38:48.500
helps detect bugs and other
issues on an ongoing basis.

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:50.960
So testing is effectively
an insurance policy

00:38:50.960 --> 00:38:52.280
against potential bugs.

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:54.470
And if your managers ever
ask, well, why should

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:55.550
we write tests?

00:38:55.550 --> 00:38:59.240
The answer is, they not only
stop those potential bugs,

00:38:59.240 --> 00:39:03.170
but they stop the business
impact of those potential bugs.

00:39:03.170 --> 00:39:05.840
Note that, also, there are
many testing options out there

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:07.820
that we reviewed-- unit
tests, function tests,

00:39:07.820 --> 00:39:10.280
system tests, so
on, and so forth,

00:39:10.280 --> 00:39:13.050
or integration tests, system
tests, so on, and so forth.

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:14.660
And they all have
different trade-offs

00:39:14.660 --> 00:39:18.720
in terms of accuracy,
breadth, speed, and cost.

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:21.650
Generally speaking, we recommend
that on critical, reused,

00:39:21.650 --> 00:39:23.810
or unmaintained
code, you have lots

00:39:23.810 --> 00:39:25.787
of tests with a variety
of different scopes.

00:39:25.787 --> 00:39:27.620
So you're going to have
a lot of unit tests,

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:29.200
you're going to have
a lot of system tests,

00:39:29.200 --> 00:39:31.325
you're going have a lot of
integration tests, maybe

00:39:31.325 --> 00:39:34.210
several static tests,
so on, and so forth.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:38.120
On less important, temporary,
or well-maintained code,

00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:41.120
you can usually get away with
fewer higher-level tests.

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:44.030
Because generally speaking, if
your code is well-maintained

00:39:44.030 --> 00:39:46.610
and you know there's a bug,
you can go in and triage it

00:39:46.610 --> 00:39:47.510
pretty quickly.

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:49.160
But if you don't
know there's a bug,

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:51.620
then there's a
problem, because if you

00:39:51.620 --> 00:39:54.260
don't know that the problem
exists, you can't address it.

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:56.760
So that's why those higher-level
tests are important to say,

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.490
well, does my code work at all?

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:00.350
And if my code works
from end to end,

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:02.180
then I'm reasonably
confident that there

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:04.042
isn't going to be a bug in it.

00:40:04.042 --> 00:40:06.000
But if the system tests
fail, then you can say,

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:07.706
OK, well something's broken.

00:40:07.706 --> 00:40:09.080
I don't know what,
but at least I

00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:11.030
can go deeper, and
take a look at it,

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:14.800
and figure out what went wrong.

00:40:14.800 --> 00:40:17.480
Now, the third takeaway
here is that Stackdriver

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.000
increases your
chances of detecting

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:21.830
production bugs quickly.

00:40:21.830 --> 00:40:23.540
Emphasis on increases
your chances,

00:40:23.540 --> 00:40:25.520
there is no guarantee
you will detect them

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:27.810
within a certain amount of time.

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:30.650
But it's easier to detect
them and more likely

00:40:30.650 --> 00:40:33.110
that you will detect them
if Stackdriver is present.

00:40:33.110 --> 00:40:36.530
The broad configuration is to
check for abnormal error rates

00:40:36.530 --> 00:40:38.420
and execution times.

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:39.875
It's pretty easy
to set up again,

00:40:39.875 --> 00:40:41.750
like it would probably
take a developer maybe

00:40:41.750 --> 00:40:45.320
about an hour or two, at most.

00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:47.800
And finally, note that tests
must address Cloud-specific

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:48.890
challenges.

00:40:48.890 --> 00:40:51.250
So the challenge of
eventual consistency,

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:53.830
you can address that using
either timeouts or exponential

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:54.769
back off.

00:40:54.769 --> 00:40:56.560
And then the challenge
of shared resources,

00:40:56.560 --> 00:40:59.470
you can address this by using
temporary test environments

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:02.680
or temporary test resources like
we did with that UUID function

00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:05.870
in the system test example.

00:41:05.870 --> 00:41:08.450
So I'd like to thank you for
coming to Next today and coming

00:41:08.450 --> 00:41:09.710
to my talk.

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:11.930
If you have any questions,
feel free to email me

00:41:11.930 --> 00:41:13.880
at faast@google.com.

00:41:13.880 --> 00:41:16.162
That is the word
faast, with two As.

00:41:16.162 --> 00:41:18.370
Otherwise, I'll be hanging
around the serverless demo

00:41:18.370 --> 00:41:18.870
both.

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:20.930
So you're welcome to come
and speak to me there.

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:21.830
Thanks.

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:23.030
